WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.767
FastText.

2
00:00:02.067 --> 00:00:04.870
Word2Vec crashes on a word it has never seen.

3
00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:08.233
FastText doesn't, and the trick is beautifully simple.

4
00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:14.700
FastText, from Facebook in twenty sixteen, rebuilds Word2Vec from character n-grams.

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.267
A word's vector is simply the sum of the vectors of its pieces, three to six characters long.

6
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:22.775
Take the word anomaly.

7
00:00:22.775 --> 00:00:24.796
We slice it into character pieces.

8
00:00:24.796 --> 00:00:28.067
The angle brackets mark where the word starts and ends.

9
00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:32.633
Each piece owns a vector, and adding them up gives the vector for the whole word.

10
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:34.833
Now the payoff.

11
00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:38.600
A misspelled word like telemetrry was never seen in training.

12
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.733
But it shares most of its pieces with the correct spelling.

13
00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:45.211
So its summed vector lands right next to telemetry.

14
00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.267
Meaning is assembled from pieces.

15
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:49.700
Here's the contrast.

16
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.781
Word2Vec stores one vector per whole word.

17
00:00:52.781 --> 00:00:54.833
FastText sums character pieces.

18
00:00:55.133 --> 00:01:00.700
An unseen word makes Word2Vec throw a key error, while FastText returns something sensible.

19
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.267
That helps most in morphology-rich languages like Tamil, Finnish, and German compounds, where word forms explode.

20
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:11.567
FastText also ships a brutally fast classifier.

21
00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:14.067
Let's prove it in code.

22
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:17.700
We train FastText and Word2Vec on the same sentences.

23
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.133
We ask FastText for a made-up word, and it happily returns a vector.

24
00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:26.467
Word2Vec has never seen that exact string, so it raises a key error.

25
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:28.900
Here's where it matters.

26
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.500
Astronaut and controller notes are full of typos and ad hoc jargon.

27
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:40.233
A FastText index still places telemetrry dropouts next to the real entries, because the pieces overlap.

28
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:44.000
The same query kills a Word2Vec index with a key error.

29
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.007
Let's recap.

30
00:01:46.007 --> 00:01:49.033
FastText builds words from character n-grams.

31
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:52.700
Unseen words and typos still get a sensible vector.

32
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.467
It shines on morphology-rich languages.

33
00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:58.308
And it's a fast classifier baseline.

34
00:01:58.308 --> 00:01:59.367
Next up: GloVe.
